---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="RESC container (version 3.11)"
  description="Konami's named-entry archive. 48-byte TOC entries with reversed-ASCII names. Used by data/recipe/recipedata.lzs (the cooking recipe database) and a handful of other named-entry containers."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'File Formats', href: `${base}/field-guide/file-formats/` },
    { label: 'RESC' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    Variant of <a href={`${base}/field-guide/file-formats/reso/`}>RESO</a> that
    stores named TOC entries instead of type tags. Each entry is 48 bytes —
    that's what distinguishes RESC v3.11 from RESO v1.21 (which uses 16-byte
    entries for variant 0). The format is <strong>fully reverse-engineered
    and round-trip verified</strong> as of step-294 (2026-05-20); the
    canonical spec for the writer lives at
    <code>notes/format_resc_v311.md</code> in the translation repo.
  </p>

  <h2>Where RESC v3.11 appears</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong><code>data/recipe/recipedata.lzs</code></strong> — the cooking recipe database. 34 entries spanning 10 category labels, 11 recipe names, and 13 "Mysterious Soup" placeholder slots. This is the canonical RESC v3.11 surface and the one the v2.4 recipe-menu translation work targeted.</li>
    <li><strong><code>data/mailorder.lzs</code></strong>, <strong><code>data/goodslist.lzs</code></strong> — RESC v3.11 graphics containers (per step-305 UI container audit). No translatable text; they hold tile / palette / cell data for the mail-order and goods-list UIs.</li>
    <li>Other one-off RESC v3.11 containers may appear in the asset tree; the writer in <code>src/archive/_resc_v311_writer.py</code> handles them with the same round-trip-safe path used for <code>recipedata.lzs</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Outer wrapper (recipedata.lzs only)</h2>
  <p>
    On disk, <code>recipedata.lzs</code> is the SZL outer wrapper
    (<code>1.00</code> magic + 9 zero bytes) followed by a 4-byte file tag
    and an LZ11 stream. The file tag at SZL <code>+0x0C..+0x10</code> is
    <strong>file-specific</strong> for <code>recipedata.lzs</code>:
    <code>b'\x00ser'</code> (NOT the default <code>b'sfo'</code> that
    <code>lzs.compress()</code> emits). The writer preserves the original
    4-byte wrapper word byte-for-byte. Other RESC v3.11 containers carry
    their own file-specific tags.
  </p>

  <h2>RESC v3.11 header (16 bytes)</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Offset</th><th>Size</th><th>Type</th><th>Field</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>0x00</code></td><td>4</td><td><code>char[4]</code></td><td>magic</td><td><code>"RESC"</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x04</code></td><td>4</td><td><code>u32 LE</code></td><td>file_count</td><td>Number of TOC entries (recipedata = 2)</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x08</code></td><td>4</td><td><code>char[4]</code></td><td>version</td><td><code>"3.11"</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>0x0C</code></td><td>4</td><td><code>u32 LE</code></td><td>variant</td><td>recipedata = 0 ("type-tagged"). Variant 1 ("simple") is theoretically possible by analogy with RESO but not observed in this ROM.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>TOC entry (48 bytes, variant 0)</h2>
  <p>
    Stride is <strong>0x30 (48 bytes)</strong> — twice the entry size of
    RESO variant 0. Begins at file offset <code>0x10</code>.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Offset</th><th>Size</th><th>Field</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>+0x00</code></td><td>16</td><td><code>type_name</code></td><td>UTF-8 name, byte-reversed within each 8-byte word. recipedata entry 0 = <code>"recipelist"</code>, entry 1 = <code>"recipepos"</code>.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>+0x10</code></td><td>4</td><td><code>unk</code></td><td>Observed zero in all entries.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>+0x14</code></td><td>4</td><td><code>subtype_tag</code></td><td>4-byte ASCII reversed (same encoding as RESO type_tag). Entry 0 stores <code>b"tlcr"</code> (reads as <code>"rclt"</code>); entry 1 stores <code>b"\x00tad"</code> (reads as <code>"dat\x00"</code>).</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>+0x18</code></td><td>4</td><td><code>length</code></td><td>Payload bytes.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>+0x1C</code></td><td>4</td><td><code>data_offset</code></td><td>Absolute offset within the decompressed file.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>+0x20</code></td><td>12</td><td>(padding)</td><td>Zero.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>+0x2C</code></td><td>4</td><td><code>fingerprint</code></td><td><code>0x7C952298</code> in both recipedata entries — looks like a build-tool watermark, not load-bearing. The writer preserves it verbatim.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h3>type_name byte-reversal convention</h3>
  <p>
    The 16-byte <code>type_name</code> field stores the human-readable name
    in 8-byte words, byte-reversed within each word. For "recipelist": word
    0 holds <code>"recipeli"</code> stored as
    <code>69 6c 65 70 69 63 65 72</code>; word 1 holds <code>"st"</code>
    plus zero padding, stored as <code>00 00 00 00 00 00 74 73</code>.
    Reverse each word and you get the human-readable name. The writer is
    robust to this only at decode time — it doesn't need to write new
    type_name values because they're per-entry-type and unchanged across
    patches.
  </p>

  <h2>recipedata.lzs payload layout</h2>
  <p>
    Two entries: <strong>entry 0 "recipelist"</strong> is the recipe-menu
    UI strings (890 bytes), and <strong>entry 1 "recipepos"</strong> is
    binary recipe metadata (1,260 bytes — item IDs, quantities, slot
    positions; no translatable text).
  </p>

  <h3>Entry 0 "recipelist" — string table</h3>
  <pre><code>{`+0x00  u32       record_count = 34
+0x04  N records of stride 12:
         +0x00  u32   text_offset (relative to start of entry-0 payload)
         +0x04  u8    recipe_id
         +0x05  u8    sub_id
         +0x06  u8    category_code
         +0x07  u8    subtype_code
         +0x08  u8    flag
         +0x09  u8    unk (always 0xff)
         +0x0a  u16   pad (always 0)
+0x04 + N*12  ... UTF-16-LE string blob, each string NUL-terminated`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    34 records. Strings are heavily <strong>deduplicated</strong>: records
    21..33 (recipe_ids 0x64..0x70 — the 13 hidden Mysterious Soup
    placeholder slots) all share one string offset at
    <code>0x36a</code> = <code>"ふしぎなスープ"</code>. The writer preserves
    deduplication: when two output strings are byte-identical, only one copy
    is emitted in the blob and both records point at it.
  </p>

  <h3>Entry 1 "recipepos" — binary metadata</h3>
  <p>
    1,260 bytes of pure binary recipe metadata — item_id refs, quantities,
    slot positions, ingredient combinations. <strong>No translatable
    strings.</strong> The writer copies this entry's bytes verbatim.
  </p>

  <h2>Alignment / padding</h2>
  <p>
    Entry-0 payload ends at <code>entry0_off + entry0_len</code>. Entry-1
    payload starts at the next 0x10-byte-aligned offset. After entry-1's
    payload ends, the decompressed file is padded to the next 0x10-byte
    boundary with zeros. For <code>recipedata.lzs</code>: entry-0 ends at
    <code>0x3EA</code>, entry-1 starts at <code>0x3F0</code> (6 zero bytes
    of pad); entry-1 ends at <code>0x8DC</code>, file ends at
    <code>0x8E0</code> (4 bytes of trailing pad). Both alignments are
    enforced by the writer.
  </p>

  <h2>Round-trip safety</h2>
  <p>
    Calling the writer with an empty substitution map produces a
    decompressed output that is byte-identical to the input — verified in
    the writer's self-test (step-294). When substitutions are made, the
    string blob is rebuilt with deduplication preserved, each entry-0
    record's <code>text_offset</code> is recomputed, entry-1's bytes pass
    through unchanged, and the final file is re-padded to 0x10 alignment.
  </p>

  <h2>Source</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>notes/format_resc_v311.md</code> — canonical writer spec (step-294, 2026-05-20).</li>
    <li><code>src/archive/_resc_v311_writer.py</code> — round-trip-safe writer.</li>
    <li><code>notes/_recipes_format_notes.md</code> — earlier partial reverse-engineering notes (kept for historical context).</li>
    <li><code>src/archive/lzs.py</code> — the SZL + LZ11 wrapper handler.</li>
  </ul>
</FieldGuideLayout>
